{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"plan-ux-research-roadmap","kind":"prompt","title":"Plan a UX research roadmap","description":"Plans a quarter or half-year of UX research from the team's open questions, prioritising by decision value and risk, matching methods to capacity and leaving room for urgent requests.","category":"ux-research","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan"],"role":["ux-researcher","product-manager","designer"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","notes"],"output":["plan","table"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"standard","interaction":"one-shot","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","depth":"specific","tags":["research-roadmap","research-prioritisation","research-ops","capacity-planning","research-questions"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["build-research-repository","write-discovery-research-plan","ux-research-study-track"],"personas":["ux-researcher"]},"args":[{"name":"questions","description":"Open questions and research requests from teams, with what decision each would inform and when that decision is due, if known.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"capacity","description":"Researchers available, share of time for research, budget for recruitment and incentives, and the period to plan.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"existing","description":"Research already done or in the repository that may answer some questions. Optional.","type":"text"}],"output_contract":{"format":"markdown","sections":["Questions triaged","Priorities","Roadmap","Capacity check","Not doing","How requests come in"]},"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"<context>\nResearch teams drown in requests when every question gets a study. A roadmap starts from the decisions the organisation faces, ranks questions by how much the answer could change a decision and how costly being wrong would be, checks the repository first, and matches each question to the lightest method that will answer it. It deliberately reserves capacity for urgent requests and continuous discovery, and says out loud what is not being done. Product decisions with dates beat curiosity, but a small slice for foundational research keeps the team from only validating.\n</context>\n\n<task>\n<questions>\n{{questions}}\n</questions>\n\n<capacity>\n{{capacity}}\n</capacity>\n{{#existing}}\n\n<existing>\n{{existing}}\n</existing>\n{{/existing}}\n\n1. Questions triaged: rewrite each request as a research question tied to a decision, mark answered or partly answered by existing work, and merge duplicates.\n2. Priorities: score each on decision impact, risk of being wrong, timing and evidence gap; show the scores and reasoning.\n3. Roadmap: by month or sprint, each study with method, rough size (participants, weeks), owner and the decision date it feeds.\n4. Capacity check: total effort against capacity, with about 20% reserved for urgent requests; flag over-commitment.\n5. Not doing: questions deferred or answered another way (analytics, desk research, self-serve methods for designers).\n6. How requests come in: a short intake template and review cadence.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not invent decisions, dates or past findings; mark unknowns.\n- Effort estimates are rough ranges labelled as such.\n- Prefer the lightest method that answers the question.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Questions triaged\n| # | Research question | Decision | Due | Existing evidence |\n## Priorities\n| # | Impact | Risk | Timing | Gap | Rank |\n## Roadmap\n## Capacity check\n## Not doing\n## How requests come in\n</output_format>","steps":[]}
